\section{Which ICC this is}{
This is the ANOVA ICC(1) of the multilevel-aggregation literature,
numerically the same quantity as Shrout & Fleiss's (1979) \code{ICC(1,1)}. It
is \strong{not} the psychometric inter-rater \code{ICC(2)}/\code{ICC(3)} family that
\code{psych::ICC()} reports, which requires a crossed design (the same raters
rating every target) that nested groups do not have. Nor is it a
restricted-maximum-likelihood variance share from a fitted
\code{y ~ 1 + (1 | group)} model: the two agree on balanced data and differ
under imbalance.

It is also \strong{not} the same as \code{\link[=netis_variance_components]{netis_variance_components()}}, which is a
\emph{conditional} share computed from the fitted model. The two answer
different questions and can differ substantially; a large ICC(1) beside a
small conditional share says the predictors account for much of the
original between-group heterogeneity.
}

\section{Negative values}{
When \code{MSB <= MSW} the ANOVA estimate is zero or negative. It is returned as
computed, with \code{status = "boundary"}: the sample shows no evidence of
between-group variance, and replacing the estimate by zero would change
the quantity Bliese defines.
}

\section{What it is evidence for}{
ICC(1) is evidence relevant to whether aggregating to the group level is
defensible. It does not settle it: theory, construct definition, and
within-group agreement (see \code{\link[=rwg]{rwg()}}) all bear on that decision, and a high
agreement index can coexist with low between-group differentiation.
}

